# grep Command

`grep` is used to search for a specific word or pattern inside files.

---

## Basic Usage

```bash
grep "word" file.txt


| Command | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `grep "text" file.txt` | search for text in a file |
| `grep -i "text" file.txt` | ignore case (case-insensitive) |
| `grep -n "text" file.txt` | show line numbers |
| `grep "text" file1 file2` | search


## grep Examples Table

| Command | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `grep "^root" /etc/passwd` | matches lines that **start** with "root" |
| `grep "bash$" /etc/passwd` | matches lines that **end** with "bash" |
| `grep "[0-9]" file.txt` | matches lines containing **any digit** |
| `grep "cat" file.txt` | matches lines containing the exact word "cat" |
| `grep "a.b" file.txt` | matches "a" followed by any single character, then "b" |
| `grep -E "ab+" file.txt` | matches "a" followed by **one or more b's** (extended regex) |
| `grep -i "word" file.txt` | **case-insensitive** match |
| `grep -n "word" file.txt` | show **line numbers** of matches |
| `grep -r "word" folder/` | search **recursively** in folder |

